<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lift Every Voice and Sing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first recited <a href="/years/1900">1900</a>; set to music <a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Lift Every Voice and Sing&quot; is a hymn written by James Weldon Johnson (1871–1938) and set to music by his brother J. Rosamond Johnson (1873–1954). After graduating from Atlanta University in 1894, James Weldon Johnson returned to his hometown of Jacksonville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, as principal of Stanton High School, the first public high school for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in the state.</p></div>
